        "viewport": "reactions",
        "type": "reactions",
        "device": "ar_headset_device",
        "location": "trajectory_analysis_zone",
        "predicate": "projectile_trajectory_animation",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Observing the Parabolic Trajectory",
          "description": "The path you see is a parabola\u2014the mathematical curve created by combining two independent motions: constant horizontal velocity and uniformly accelerated vertical motion (due to gravity, g \u2248 9.8 m/s\u00b2 downward). At the peak of the trajectory, the vertical velocity component Vy equals zero, but the horizontal component Vx is still 14.1 m/s. This is the moment of maximum height. The range (horizontal distance traveled) depends on both Vx and the time of flight, which is determined by the vertical motion. This demonstrates the independence principle: the horizontal and vertical motions are independent\u2014gravity only affects the vertical motion, not the horizontal."
        },
