        "instruction": {
          "title": "How Velocity Changes Over the Flight",
          "description": "Let's trace how the velocity components change throughout the projectile's flight. At launch (t=0): Vx = 14.1 m/s (horizontal), Vy = 14.1 m/s (upward). As the projectile rises, Vx stays constant at 14.1 m/s, but Vy decreases due to gravity at a rate of 9.8 m/s per second. At the peak (when Vy = 0), Vx is still 14.1 m/s. During descent, Vy becomes negative (downward) and grows more negative (faster downward). At impact, Vy has the same magnitude as at launch but points downward. The key insight: the horizontal velocity never changes, while the vertical velocity steadily changes under the influence of gravity. This separation of concerns makes projectile motion predictable and calculable."
        },
